The problem is that you're using the Firebase Token Generator library, which is deprecated. It's not the same as the Firebase Admin SDK. The Firebase Token Generator is for generating custom tokens, but it's not the same as the Firebase Admin SDK. The Firebase Admin SDK is for server-side code, and it can generate custom tokens.
So, to generate a Firebase token, you should use the Firebase Admin SDK. Here's how you can do it:
First, you need to install the Firebase Admin SDK for PHP. You can do this by running:
composer require google/cloud-firestore
Then, you need to set up the Firebase Admin SDK in your Laravel app. You can do this by adding the following code to your app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php file:
use Illuminate\Support\ServiceProvider;
use Google\Cloud\Firestore\FirestoreClient;

class AppServiceProvider extends ServiceProvider
{
    public function boot()
    {
        $firestore = new FirestoreClient([
            'projectId' => env('FIRESTORE_PROJECT_ID'),
        ]);
        app()->instance(FirestoreClient::class, $firestore);
    }
}
Then, in your controller, you can use the Firebase Admin SDK to generate a custom token. Here's an example:
use Google\Cloud\Firestore\FirestoreClient;

public function loginWithFirebase()
{
    $user = User::where('email', 'test@example.com')->first();

    $firestore = app(FirestoreClient::class);

    $customToken = $firestore->createCustomToken($user->id);

    return response()->json(['token' => $customToken]);
}
This should generate a custom token that you can use to authenticate with Firebase.
Note that you need to have the Firebase Admin SDK initialized with the correct credentials. You can do this by downloading the service account key JSON file from the Firebase console and setting the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS environment variable to point to it.
Also, make sure that the user exists in Firebase Authentication. If the user doesn't exist, the createCustomToken method will throw an error.
So, to summarize, you should use the Firebase Admin SDK to generate a custom token, not the Firebase Token Generator library.
